<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ceolfrith</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 642–716</span> · Also known as Saint Ceolfrith</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ceolfrith (c. 642–716) was abbot of the twin monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth–Jarrow</a> from 688 and the teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>. He enlarged the library founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> and had three great single-volume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Bibles made at the monastery. In 716 he set out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> carrying one of them, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, as a gift for the pope, but died on the way at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langres">Langres</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ceolfrith.jpg" alt="Ceolfrith" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mark Smiles · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Pauls_Monastery_Jarrow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
